vendored:       2026-06-20

PYTHON FLOOR — load-bearing pin: 2024.06 is the LAST GEF release that supports
Python 3.6–3.9 (`PYTHON_MIN_VERSION = (3, 6)`, no `match` statements). GEF
2025.01+ requires Python 3.10 and uses 3.10 `match`/`case` syntax. Plain `gdb`
on PATH is pwndbg's bundled GDB 17 (Python 3.13), but the system /usr/bin/gdb
embeds Python 3.6 (EL8) or 3.9 (EL9), and current GEF fails to *parse* there
(SyntaxError, a traceback on every start). DO NOT bump past 2024.06 while any
managed host's /usr/bin/gdb has Python < 3.10. Verify a bump with the SYSTEM gdb
(plain `gdb` would test pwndbg's Python 3.13 and pass falsely):
  /usr/bin/gdb -nx -batch -ex 'source ~/.config/gdb/gef.py' -ex 'gef help'
— it must print "GEF for linux ready ... using Python engine 3.6/3.9", NOT a Python
SyntaxError. (`gef-legacy` is NOT a fallback — it targets GDB+Python2.) pwndbg,
the other front-end, is immune: its portable build bundles a newer Python.

files:          gef.py  (single self-contained script; verbatim upstream, zero hand-edits)
note:           Leading-dot filename (.vendor) → excluded by the symlink-each entry's
                `exclude` list; provenance only, never deployed.
                gef.py deploys 0644 to ~/.config/gdb/gef.py and is sourced by the tracked
                ~/.gdbinit (dotfiles/gdbinit.tera) so plain `gdb` loads the GEF UI.
                Linux-only: both ~/.gdbinit and ~/.config/gdb are declared only in
                config.linux.toml, so they never deploy on Windows. The OTHER GDB
                front-end, pwndbg (`github:pwndbg/pwndbg` in config.linux.toml), does NOT
                read ~/.gdbinit; its bundled gdb is the one plain `gdb` runs (first on
                PATH), so plain `gdb` = GDB 17 + GEF and `pwndbg` = pwndbg. No config.toml [vars] entry — pinned only here.
                Bump (ONLY once the fleet's gdb-Python ≥ the target GEF's floor — see PYTHON
                FLOOR above): re-download gef.py at the new tag, refresh release + asset +
                file-sha256 + vendored, then re-verify the load on an EL9 host. ~/.gdbinit
                needs no change.
